// A struct-body CONSTANT read off an IMPORTED type, through both
// spellings: `Box.LIMIT` (the type's name) and `b.LIMIT` (a value of
// it). One value per type, so both lines print the same number -- and
// the number is u64.MAX on purpose.
//
// This is the shape that bit this tree before: `Type.NAME` folded but
// `value.NAME` fell through to the ordinary field path and emitted C
// naming a member the struct does not declare, so the failure was cc's,
// not Zen's. A constant has no storage; folding is the only correct
// answer from either side of the dot. Across an import boundary is the
// harder half: the constant's declaration lives in another module.
//
// One-line breaker: fold constants only when the type is declared in
// the same module -> cc rejects the emitted C (member does not exist).

Box = box.box

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, IoError> {
    b = Box();
    println("{}", Box.LIMIT);
    println("{}", b.LIMIT);
    Ok(0);
}
